<p>Spain’s government pushed back on a White House assertion that Madrid had agreed to cooperate with U.S. military operations tied to the war in Iran, including possible use of Spanish bases, saying no such agreement exists. The public contradiction sharpened a diplomatic rift, with each side offering a different account of what, if anything, Spain has authorized. Prime Minister Pedro Sánchez repeated Spain’s opposition to the war and said the country will not back actions it sees as damaging and against its values and interests. President Donald Trump, according to multiple reports, threatened to halt trade with Spain and called Madrid a “terrible” ally—pushing the clash beyond security cooperation into economic pressure and alliance politics.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/Irg4dOsYFDGF86PmbnQOkMF29BMqk6XDIxMnYxIKtrzIBSqXODAz59N_t5RPHDn7ZXw5sNSC7Mvsw6nq1HkuYkbDlIFlOMI4ipCPe5WKwJ5WyZ-7zysiKiCW7VJv6SZl_iuzU56FbOiLbBwCLHxEm2U_XlmwohAvxafcHNS4XqpYt-TOFdWe5bcGOLGp_t3V3fzXbb2NgIftHw-aNH148A' alt='Pedro Sánchez, Spain’s prime minister, has condemned the attack on Iran as a violation of international law.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Escalation in tone: Sánchez’s stance drew a personal rebuke from Trump, who called Spain a “terrible” ally and paired the criticism with threats of economic retaliation.</li><li>How it surfaced: The clash unfolded in a rapid, public back-and-forth of official statements—especially on Wednesday—rather than around a jointly described, documented agreement.</li><li>Retaliation framing: Sánchez cast the issue as resisting pressure, saying Spain should not change course “out of fear of retaliation,” and repeating a “no to war” message in response to U.S. threats.</li></ul><blockquote>We will not be complicit in something that is harmful to the world and contrary to our values and interests, simply out of fear of retaliation. - Pedro Sánchez</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Spanish government (Pedro Sánchez): Sánchez reiterated Spain’s opposition to the Iran war, denied any agreement to support U.S. operations from Spanish territory, and pushed back after Trump called Spain a “terrible” ally. (<a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/spain-denies-us-claim-of-military-cooperation-on-iran-as-rift-deepens/article70704863.ece'>The Hindu</a>)</li><li>White House: The White House said Spain had agreed to cooperate with the U.S. military, including on operations linked to the Middle East conflict—an assertion Madrid rejected. (<a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/europe/white-house-spain-has-agreed-to-cooperate-with-us-military'>The Straits Times</a>)</li><li>U.S. and Spanish officials (process/accountability angle): Reporting highlighted how the dispute intensified as officials traded dueling public statements over whether U.S. forces could use Spanish bases for Middle East operations, underscoring a widening diplomatic tussle. (<a href='https://www.pbs.org/newshour/world/spain-denies-cooperating-with-u-s-military-operations-in-middle-east-contradicting-white-house'>PBS NewsHour</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/spain-denies-us-claim-of-military-cooperation-on-iran-as-rift-deepens/article70704863.ece'>Spain denies U.S. claim of military cooperation on Iran as rift deepens</a> - thehindu.com</li><li><a href='https://www.pbs.org/newshour/world/spain-denies-cooperating-with-u-s-military-operations-in-middle-east-contradicting-white-house'>Spain denies cooperating with U.S. military operations in Middle East, contradicting White House</a> - pbs.org</li><li><a href='https://www.nytimes.com/live/2026/03/04/world/iran-war-israel-lebanon-trump/spain-white-house'>Spain insists it is not cooperating with the U.S. on Iran war despite a White House claim.</a> - nytimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/europe/white-house-spain-has-agreed-to-cooperate-with-us-military'>White House says Spain has agreed to 